# Publishing {{ algorithm_name }}

One PyPI distribution, `{{ package_name }}`, contains all declared profiles. Its Python import is
`{{ algorithm_slug }}` and its executable is `{{ algorithm_slug | replace('_', '-') }}`. Publishing the
algorithm does not require another release of the shared core or template.

## Prepare once

1. Choose an available PyPI name; edit `[project].name` in `pyproject.toml` if needed. The import and CLI
   names need not match it. Update this guide and the CLI installation hint if changing the name later.
2. Set `project.urls.Repository`, choose an SPDX `project.license` expression compatible with your
   implementation and upstream sources, and add the actual `LICENSE` file. Review the authors,
   description and README. The template deliberately does not choose a license for you.
3. Declare every runtime dependency in `pyproject.toml`; release dependencies must be installable
   from the package index, without Git/direct URL requirements. Commit `uv.lock` after `uv lock`.
4. Complete every declared profile and its scientific tests; remove completed profiles from
   `STUB_PROFILES`. Publication rejects remaining stubs. Passing packaging checks does not establish
   scientific correctness. Extend `tests/wheel_smoke.py` for required algorithm resources/configuration.
5. Merge `.github/workflows/publish.yml` into `main`. Create GitHub environments `testpypi` and `pypi`;
   configure release reviewers/tag restrictions as appropriate for your repository.
6. On [PyPI](https://pypi.org/manage/account/publishing/) and separately on
   [TestPyPI](https://test.pypi.org/manage/account/publishing/), register a pending GitHub publisher:

   | Field | Value |
   |---|---|
   | PyPI project name | `{{ package_name }}` |
   | Owner | Your GitHub user or organization |
   | Repository | This algorithm's GitHub repository name |
   | Workflow filename | `publish.yml` |
   | Environment | `pypi` on PyPI; `testpypi` on TestPyPI |

   For an existing PyPI project, add the publisher in its Publishing settings instead. No API token
   is needed. See the [official setup guide](https://docs.pypi.org/trusted-publishers/creating-a-project-through-oidc/).

## Check each release

Update `[project].version` and `CHANGELOG.md`, then run these commands from the repository root
(Bash, with Git and uv installed):

```bash
uv lock
uv sync --locked --extra test
uv run --no-sync pytest -rs
uv run --no-sync python scripts/check_release.py
bash scripts/check_distribution.sh
```

The distribution check builds a wheel from the source archive, validates metadata, installs the base
wheel in a fresh environment, checks imports/commands/resources from a temporary working directory,
then installs orchestration and checks a preprocessing dry-run. It runs no training or SLURM jobs.
It leaves the checked files in `dist/`; move old `dist/` contents elsewhere before repeating it.
Commit the version, lockfile, changelog and implementation changes; merge passing CI into `main`.

## Tag, rehearse, publish

Replace `OWNER/REPOSITORY` below. Set `version` to the exact version in `pyproject.toml`:

```bash
repository=OWNER/REPOSITORY
version=0.1.0
git switch main
git pull --ff-only
git tag -a "v$version" -m "Release $version"
git push origin "v$version"
gh workflow run publish.yml --repo "$repository" --ref "v$version" -f index=testpypi
gh run list --repo "$repository" --workflow publish.yml
```

Wait for success and verify installation from TestPyPI. Install dependencies from PyPI first, then
replace only this package from TestPyPI, avoiding mixed-index dependency resolution:

```bash
uv venv /tmp/algorithm-release-check
uv pip install --python /tmp/algorithm-release-check/bin/python dist/*.whl
uv pip install --python /tmp/algorithm-release-check/bin/python --reinstall --no-deps \
  --index-url https://test.pypi.org/simple/ "{{ package_name }}==$version"
/tmp/algorithm-release-check/bin/python -I tests/wheel_smoke.py
gh workflow run publish.yml --repo "$repository" --ref "v$version" -f index=pypi
gh run list --repo "$repository" --workflow publish.yml
```

The workflow requires the matching version tag on `main` history, a committed lockfile, complete
profiles, passing tests, and valid installed distributions. A separate job uploads those exact checked
files using Trusted Publishing. Rehearsal and production runs each build and check their own artifacts
from the same tag. After success, install `{{ package_name }}==$version` in a fresh environment and
repeat the smoke check. Never move a published tag or reuse a published version; increment it for fixes.

## Registering your algorithm

After publication, register the package in the shared ecosystem's
[algorithm catalogue](https://mcdermotthealthai.github.io/meds-algorithm/implemented-algorithms/).
Open a PR against `catalogue/algorithms.yaml` in `McDermottHealthAI/meds-algorithm`
with the PyPI name `{{ package_name }}`, exact published
version, immutable implementation commit, declared profiles and links to validation evidence. The site
generates the installation command from that reviewed pin. An implementation under review can be listed
earlier with `pypi_version: null`; do not advertise an install command for an unpublished name.
For a private repository, authorize only metadata intended for public listing and mark access private.
Update the entry when a new release has been reviewed; automated release discovery does not establish
scientific validation. Keep dataset contents and private run details out of the public catalogue.

## What installation includes

`configs/runs/` and `algorithms/` are the single editable source for portable run defaults and MEDS-DEV
YAMLs. The wheel copies them to `{{ algorithm_slug }}/resources/` during its build; the source archive
includes the originals. No second copy is maintained in Git.

The runner uses `./configs/runs` when present, otherwise its installed defaults. `--config-dir` explicitly
selects user configuration. Supply dataset paths and run settings as overrides or external configuration;
do not put dataset-specific paths, credentials, labels, datasets or fitted weights in bundled defaults.
The source archive uses an explicit file allowlist; review its contents before publishing.

Access installed profile YAMLs with `importlib.resources.files("{{ algorithm_slug }}") / "resources" /
"algorithms"`. SLURM shell scripts and implementation reports remain checkout tooling. Large pretrained
weights should be retrieved as separately versioned artifacts, not silently embedded in a wheel.
